---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/jakejarvis/Clicker";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — Clicker"
  description="What Clicker and this website do with your data."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated October 10, 2026</p>

    <p>
      Clicker is a free, open-source Apple TV remote for macOS, made by Jake
      Jarvis. This policy covers the Clicker app and this website,
      clicker.jarv.is. The short version: the app has no accounts, no analytics
      and no server of its own. Everything it knows stays on your Mac, and the
      only thing it sends to the internet is a check for updates.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app sends over your network</h2>
    <p>
      Clicker finds Apple TVs with Bonjour on your local network and talks to
      them directly using Apple's Companion protocol, the same one the Apple TV
      Remote on an iPhone uses. After pairing, that connection is encrypted end
      to end between your Mac and the Apple TV. Button presses, text you type
      into the TV's search and password fields, volume changes and app launches
      go only to the Apple TV you're controlling. They never pass through a
      server run by Clicker or anyone else.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you choose Rescan, Clicker also briefly opens and closes a few
      connections to the TV's last known address to wake it, without sending
      anything. This also stays on your local network.
    </p>

    <h2>Update checks</h2>
    <p>
      Clicker uses the open-source <a href="https://sparkle-project.org"
        >Sparkle</a
      > framework to check for updates once a day, and whenever you choose <strong
        >Check for Updates…</strong
      >. A check downloads the update feed from <code
        >clicker.jarv.is/appcast.xml</code
      >. Like any web request, it reveals your IP address to the server, and its
      user agent names the app, its version and Sparkle's version (for example, <code
        >Clicker/1.0 Sparkle/2.10.0</code
      >). Sparkle's optional system profiling, which would send details about
      your Mac, is turned off, so nothing else is sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      The feed is served through Vercel, which passes the request on to GitHub
      Pages, where the feed file is stored. If you install an update, the update
      itself is downloaded from GitHub Releases. Vercel and GitHub may log these
      requests as described in their own policies (see <a href="#services"
        >Services this relies on</a
      >).
    </p>
    <p>
      You can turn this off with <strong>Check Automatically</strong> in Settings.
      Clicker never installs an update without asking you first.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app stores on your Mac</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Pairing keys</strong> for each Apple TV you pair with. Release builds
        keep them in your Mac's keychain, where only Clicker can read them. Builds
        you compile yourself without signing keep them in a file in <code
          >~/Library/Application Support/Clicker</code
        > instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Preferences</strong>, in <code
          >~/Library/Preferences/com.jakejarvis.Clicker.plist</code
        >: the name this Mac shows on your Apple TV (your Mac's name unless you
        change it), a random identifier the Apple TV uses to recognize this Mac,
        which Apple TV you last used, each Apple TV's last known network
        address, the apps you recently opened and the apps you starred on each
        Apple TV, and your update settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Diagnostic logs</strong> in macOS's own system log, which record things
        like the names and addresses of Apple TVs, which buttons were pressed and
        any connection errors. The text you type isn't logged, only how many characters
        it has. These logs stay on your Mac unless you share them yourself, for example
        when you report a bug.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Forget</strong> in Settings deletes an Apple TV's pairing keys, recent
      apps, starred apps and saved address. Your Apple TV keeps its own record of
      the pairing, which you can remove under <strong
        >Settings › Remotes and Devices › Remote App and Devices</strong
      > on the TV. To remove everything, forget each Apple TV, quit Clicker, then
      delete the app and the preferences file listed above.
    </p>

    <h2>Permissions</h2>
    <p>
      The first time it runs, macOS asks whether Clicker may find devices on
      your local network. Clicker needs this to see your Apple TVs and uses it
      for nothing else. If you turn on <strong>Launch at Login</strong>, Clicker
      registers itself with macOS as a login item, which you can also manage in
      System Settings.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site is hosted on Vercel and uses Vercel Web Analytics to count
      visits. Web Analytics doesn't use cookies. It records the page you viewed,
      the referring site, your approximate location (country, region and city),
      and your browser, operating system and device type. To tell visits apart,
      Vercel uses a hash created from your request, which is discarded after 24
      hours, and the statistics Clicker sees are anonymous and aggregated.
      Vercel's <a href="https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/privacy-policy"
        >Web Analytics privacy page</a
      > has the details.
    </p>
    <p>
      Like any web host, Vercel receives your IP address and request details
      when you load a page. The changelog and download links point to GitHub,
      which applies its own policies when you follow them. This site sets no
      cookies and has no forms, accounts or mailing list.
    </p>

    <h2 id="services">Services this relies on</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Vercel</strong> hosts this website, serves the update feed and provides
        Web Analytics. See the <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy"
          >Vercel Privacy Policy</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> hosts the source code, stores the update feed (on
        GitHub Pages) and hosts the downloads (on GitHub Releases). See the <a
          href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
          >GitHub General Privacy Statement</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple</strong>: macOS itself may contact Apple, for example to
        check the app's notarization the first time you open it. That happens
        under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/"
          >Apple's privacy policy</a
        >, not because of anything Clicker does.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Clicker doesn't do</h2>
    <p>
      Clicker doesn't sell, rent or share personal information, because it
      doesn't collect any beyond the aggregate site statistics above. There's no
      advertising, no tracking across apps or websites, and no data used to
      train AI models.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Clicker and this site aren't directed at children and don't knowingly
      collect information from anyone, including children under 13.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Because the app keeps your data on your Mac, you can see and delete all of
      it yourself, as described above. Depending on where you live, you may have
      rights over data that Vercel or GitHub hold about your visits and
      downloads. Those requests go to them directly, since Clicker can't access
      their logs of individual visitors.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a
      new date. The history of every change is public in the <a
        href={`${repo}/commits/main/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}
        >site's source code</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy? Open an issue on <a href={`${repo}/issues`}
        >GitHub</a
      >.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
